# [[ .Name ]]

A [tug](https://github.com/cuonggt/tug) app: Go handlers render [[ .Framework ]] pages
through Inertia, with no API in between, and people have accounts, kept
in [[ .DatabaseName ]]. `README.md` says what each file is. This says how to
change the app as tug has it done, what not to do, and how to check a
change. tug's guide, at the version `go.mod` requires, is at
[[ .Guide ]].

## Check a change

Before saying a change is done:

```sh
[[ if .SQLite -]]
tug gen            # the TypeScript of the Go's pages and routes, which tug dev writes too
npm run typecheck  # the pages against the Go's props, routes and forms
go fmt ./...       # the Go as gofmt writes it
go vet ./...
go test ./...
[[- else -]]
docker compose up -d   # the database, which tug dev and the tests use
tug gen                # the TypeScript of the Go's pages and routes, which tug dev writes too
npm run typecheck      # the pages against the Go's props, routes and forms
go fmt ./...           # the Go as gofmt writes it
go vet ./...
go test ./...
[[- end ]]
```

`tug routes` lists the routes, each with the line that added it, and its
handler, with the guards around it. `tug build` builds the frontend into
one binary, as a deploy does. The tests visit the pages as Inertia's
client does, with `tugtest`, with no browser or build, each on a database
of its own: they're how a page is checked. `tug dev` serves the app until
it's stopped, so it answers nothing in a script, and a binary run by hand
takes `ADDR=127.0.0.1:8080`, as the default listens on every interface.

## Rules

- `resources/js/tug` is tug gen's, from the Go: never edit it. A type
  there that's wrong is changed in the Go, then written again by tug gen.
- The pages run under a Content-Security-Policy, which runs a script or a
  `<style>` only with the page's nonce, as `app.html`'s carry
  `{{ .Nonce }}`. Don't write an inline script, an `on...` attribute, or a
  `<style>` without it, or add `'unsafe-inline'` to the policy in
  `main.go`. A library that injects a style is handed the nonce, from the
  page's `<meta property="csp-nonce">`
[[- if .React ]], as `resources/js/inertia.tsx`
  hands it to Radix's dialogs, by `setNonce`
[[- end ]]. What's blocked, the browser's
  console says, and the app's log.
- A route whose handler binds input says which, with `Takes`, and its
  form's action is `form()`'s, typed by that input.
- A component added to `resources/js/components/ui`, as the README says,
  takes `cn` from `[[ if .Svelte ]]@/lib/utils.js[[ else ]]@/lib/utils[[ end ]]`, as the others there do.
[[- if .React ]] shadcn's CLI,
  since 4.x, imports it from shadcn's own `cn` package instead, and adds
  that to `package.json`: change the import, and take the package out.
[[- else ]] If its CLI
  imports it from elsewhere, as shadcn's own does since 4.x, from its
  `cn` package, which it adds to `package.json`, change the import, and
  take the package out.
[[- end ]]
- A page is laid out with the properties that name a start and an end,
  `ms-` and `me-`, `ps-` and `pe-`, `text-start`, `start-` and `end-`,
  not a left and a right, so it mirrors itself in a language written
  right to left, which the document says with `dir`. What a side can't
  say takes Tailwind's `rtl:` variant, as an icon that points:
  `rtl:rotate-180`.
- A flash key is declared in `flash.go`, with `tug.Flash`, and set through
  it.
- A route for users is behind `usersOnly`, whose handler is handed the
  user, and `verified`, for an email they've shown is theirs; what could
  hand the account to someone else, as changing its email, behind
  `passwordConfirmed` too. A guest's page is behind `guestsOnly`.
- A page's props never have a password's hash or a secret: `User`'s json
  tags leave them out, and a new field that's a secret has `json:"-"`.
- A handler's writes are in one transaction, `a.inTx`'s, and so are the
  jobs it pushes about them, as mail, so they're kept together, or not at
  all. No slow work in it, as hashing a password, which holds up the
  other writes.
- Mail goes by a job, never in the request: a mail server that's down
  then holds nothing up, and the job runs again later.
- A migration that has run is never changed: the app stops as it starts
  at one whose SQL has. A change is a new one.
- An error says what's wrong to the person who gets it, as "title is
  required": no Go types, and no raw errors. What the app says to a person
  goes through `c.T`, for `tug lang` to find, to translate.
[[- if .SSR ]]
- A page renders on the server too, in Node: no `window` or `document` at
  a module's top, or before it's mounted.
[[- end ]]

## Add a page

1. In Go, its props, each named by its json tag, its declaration, and its
   handler, as the dashboard's in `main.go`:

   ```go
   type PostsIndexProps struct {
   	Posts []Post `json:"posts"`
   }

   var PostsIndex = tug.Page[PostsIndexProps]("Posts/Index")

   func (a *app) postsIndex(c *tug.Ctx, user *User) error {
   	posts, err := a.posts.byUser(c.Context(), user.ID)
   	if err != nil {
   		return err
   	}
   	return PostsIndex.Render(c, PostsIndexProps{Posts: posts})
   }
   ```

2. Its route, in `newApp`, named, as the frontend links to it by its name:

   ```go
   app.Get("/posts", a.usersOnly(verified(a.postsIndex))).Name("posts.index")
   ```

3. Its component, `resources/js/pages/<Name>.[[ .Component ]]`, here
   `Posts/Index`.
[[- if .Vue ]]
   Its props are `defineProps<Pages['Posts/Index'] & SharedProps>()`,
   from `@/tug/pages`, as Vue can't work out `PageProps<'Posts/Index'>`,
   and its title is Inertia's `<Head title="Posts" />`.
[[- else if .Svelte ]]
   Its props are `PageProps<'Posts/Index'>`, from `@/tug/pages`, and its
   title is `<Head title="Posts" />`, from `@/Head.svelte`.
[[- else ]]
   Its props are `PageProps<'Posts/Index'>`, from `@/tug/pages`, and its
   title is Inertia's `<Head title="Posts" />`.
[[- end ]]
   It has no layout of its own: `resources/js/inertia.[[ .Script ]]` gives each
   page one by its name, the app's for `Posts/Index`, the settings' inside
   it for `Settings/*`, and the card's for `Auth/*`. It's made of the
   components in `resources/js/components/ui`, as the other pages are, and
   the README says how to add more. A page for the header's links is added
   to `nav` in `[[ .AppLayoutFile ]]`.
4. A link to it is `route('posts.index')`, from `@/tug/routes`, which
   knows each named route, and the values of its wildcards, by name.

5. Its own words go through `useLang`, from `@/lib/lang`, as every page
   here does: `t('Save')` says a word in the request's language, and
   `choice(':count post|:count posts', n)` says one for a count. A word
   said outside the markup, as a static `layout`'s title, is marked with
   `text()`, from `@/tug/lang`, and said with `t()` where it's read.
   `tug lang vi` writes them all into `lang/<language>.json`, beside what
   the Go says, to translate. `n(1234)` and `d(post.createdAt, { dateStyle:
   'long' })` say a number and a time the way the language writes them, as
   the dashboard says the day the account was made: a time is in the app's
   zone, `APP_TIMEZONE`'s, and in the reader's own once the page is open in
   their browser.

## Add a form

1. Its input, with validate's tags, and a handler that binds it:

   ```go
   type PostInput struct {
   	Title string `json:"title" validate:"required,max=100"`
   }

   func (a *app) storePost(c *tug.Ctx, user *User) error {
   	var in PostInput
   	if err := c.BindValid(&in); err != nil {
   		return err // back to the form, with what to fix
   	}
   	if err := a.posts.add(c.Context(), user.ID, in.Title); err != nil {
   		return err
   	}
   	Success.Set(c, c.T("Saved."))
   	return c.RedirectRoute("posts.index")
   }
   ```

2. Its route, which takes it:

   ```go
   app.Post("/posts", a.usersOnly(verified(a.storePost))).Name("posts.store").Takes(PostInput{})
   ```

[[ if .React -]]
3. The form is `<Form<Inputs['posts.store']> action={form('posts.store')}>`,
   whose errors are its input's fields, as
   `resources/js/pages/Auth/ForgotPassword.tsx`'s is.
[[- else -]]
3. The form is a `<Form>` of `createForm<Inputs['posts.store']>()`, with
   `form('posts.store')` as its action, whose errors are its input's
   fields, as `resources/js/pages/Auth/ForgotPassword.[[ .Component ]]`'s is.
[[- end ]]
   Its `validate('title')`, as a field is left, has the server check it,
   by the same `BindValid`.

## Add a table

1. `tug migrate new create_posts` writes
   `migrations/<when>_create_posts.sql`: the table in [[ .DatabaseName ]]'s SQL,
   and after its `-- down` line, what undoes it. The app runs it as it
   starts, and under tug dev as it's written.
2. Its Go, as the users': the row's type, and the table's, with its
   queries in `<table>_db.go`, as `users.go` and `users_db.go` are, in
   [[ .DatabaseName ]]'s SQL, with [[ if .Postgres ]]`$1`[[ else ]]`?`[[ end ]] for each value, through its `db`, a `dbtx`,
   so they run in a transaction too:

   ```go
   type posts struct {
   	db dbtx
   }

   func (ps *posts) in(tx *sql.Tx) *posts {
   	return &posts{db: tx}
   }
   ```

3. A field of `app`'s, `posts *posts`, made in `newApp` with the others,
   `posts: &posts{db: e.DB}`.
4. Writes that go together, in `a.inTx`, through `a.posts.in(tx)`, with
   the jobs about them, as `register` in `auth.go` has the mail that
   verifies an email.

## Add a job

A job's kind is made in `newApp` with `queue.Handle`, as `verify-mail`
is, and pushed, in a handler's transaction, through `a.jobs.in(tx)`. It
carries what it needs to find its work, as IDs, and does it as it runs,
as `VerifyMail` makes its mail, link and all. A job on a schedule is
`Schedule`'s, as `prune-jobs`. The mail itself is in `mail.go`, in plain
text and HTML.

## Test it

The tests are Go's, beside the files they test, as `auth_test.go`. Each
makes the app on a database of its own, with `newTestApp`, and a user
logged in with `verifiedUser`:

```go
func TestAUserSeesTheirPosts(t *testing.T) {
	app := newTestApp(t)
	c := app.verifiedUser("Ann", "ann@example.com", "a long password")
	posts := tugtest.Props(c.Get("/posts"), PostsIndex)
	// ...
	r := c.Post("/posts", map[string]any{"title": ""})
	if errs := r.Follow().Errors(); errs["title"] != "title is required" {
		t.Fatalf("errors %v", errs)
	}
}
```

`tugtest.Props` fails the test for another page. A redirect's next page
is `r.Follow()`, its flash `r.Page.Flash`, and a guest is `app.client()`.
The mail a test's app sends is `app.outbox`'s, and
`app.outbox.NextTo(t, "ann@example.com")` waits for the next to an
address.

## Upgrade tug

`tug upgrade`, with nothing uncommitted, brings the app what the starter
has gained since the tug that made it, which `tug.json` names: it merges
the starter's changes into the app's own, and marks where both changed
the same lines, between `<<<<<<<` and `>>>>>>>`. A migration the starter
changed is listed with its diff rather than changed, as one that has run
is never changed: make the same change in a new one, with `tug migrate
new`, in [[ .DatabaseName ]]'s SQL. Put right what it marks, keeping what the
app's change was for, then check the change as above.
